{
  "overall_reason": "Verified each finding against protocol fields, testbed recipe, and evidence matrix. Rejected findings that conflate model architecture documentation with intervention contracts (M1), misunderstand literature review structure (E1), overlook existing status acknowledgments (E2), or fail to distinguish between different parameter types (E5). Uphold findings where artifact paths concretely establish scope mismatches between general claims and specific intervention targets (M2, E3) or unsupported mechanistic claims (E4).",
  "decisions": [
    {
      "finding_id": "M1",
      "status": "rejected",
      "rationale": "The operator_targets field in testbed_recipe documents the complete model parameter structure for verification purposes, not the intervention contract. The actual intervention target is explicitly defined in intervention_target (blocks.0.fc1.weight) and intervention_protocol. These fields serve distinct purposes—architecture documentation vs. intervention specification—and there is no ambiguity or execution error risk.",
      "repair_instruction": ""
    },
    {
      "finding_id": "M2",
      "status": "upheld",
      "rationale": "The title claims 'FFN Neuron Capacity Balancing' and hypothesis references 'FFN weight geometry regularization' generally, but intervention_target specifies only blocks.0.fc1.weight. The testbed_recipe.model confirms 2 blocks exist, yet only block 0's FFN layer receives regularization. This creates a scope mismatch between the general claim and specific implementation.",
      "repair_instruction": "Either extend intervention_target to include blocks.1.fc1.weight to match the general hypothesis claims, or qualify the title and hypothesis to specify 'First-Layer FFN Neuron Capacity Balancing' to accurately reflect the single-block intervention scope."
    },
    {
      "finding_id": "E1",
      "status": "rejected",
      "rationale": "Literature reviews appropriately include contextual background citations alongside domain-specific work. The gap_statement specifically identifies the FFN weight norm variance gap in time-series transformers. The broader citations (time series fundamentals, finance, neuroscience, vision) establish general context while the gap remains precisely scoped. This is standard literature review practice, not a defect.",
      "repair_instruction": ""
    },
    {
      "finding_id": "E2",
      "status": "rejected",
      "rationale": "The literature_summary field explicitly acknowledges the erratum and retracted status: 'Replication...discloses that independent reproduction confirmed inconclusive decisions' and 'Entropy-Maximized...carries a retracted notice from the journal.' The gap_statement addresses FFN weight norm variance specifically, which differs from the attention consistency and multiplicative noise interventions in the nearest-neighbor papers. The finding incorrectly claims these are referenced as foundation without status acknowledgment.",
      "repair_instruction": ""
    },
    {
      "finding_id": "E3",
      "status": "upheld",
      "rationale": "The title states 'FFN Neuron Capacity Balancing Tests Whether Weight Norm Uniformity Improves Subject-Disjoint Generalization' broadly, and the gap_statement references 'FFN weight geometry regularization' generally. However, intervention_target specifies only blocks.0.fc1.weight while testbed_recipe.model confirms 2 blocks total. This scope mismatch between general claims and single-layer intervention is concretely established by the cited artifact paths.",
      "repair_instruction": "Align title and gap_statement with actual intervention scope by either extending intervention_target to include blocks.1.fc1.weight for all FFN layers, or revising title to specify 'First-Layer FFN Neuron Capacity Balancing' to accurately reflect the blocks.0.fc1.weight-only intervention."
    },
    {
      "finding_id": "E4",
      "status": "upheld",
      "rationale": "The mechanistic_rationale field makes a causal claim: 'Uniform neuron capacity prevents over-reliance on specific features, improving robustness to subject-specific sensor noise in inertial signal classification.' The literature_summary and citations do not provide evidence specifically linking FFN weight norm variance to subject-disjoint generalization or sensor noise robustness. Citations[10] discusses activation routing, citations[24] discusses weight differences—neither supports the weight-norm-variance mechanism claimed.",
      "repair_instruction": "Reframe mechanistic_rationale as a hypothesis rather than established rationale (e.g., 'We hypothesize that uniform neuron capacity may prevent...'), or provide literature evidence specifically supporting the weight-norm-variance-to-robustness mechanism in time-series transformer contexts."
    },
    {
      "finding_id": "E5",
      "status": "rejected",
      "rationale": "The rationale states 'No prior registered study on this testbed has tested FFN weight geometry regularization.' The Spectral Entropy Minimization paper (citations[3]) tested 'input projection weights' specifically, not FFN weights. Input projections (input_proj.weight) and FFN weights (blocks.*.fc1.weight) are distinct parameter types in the transformer architecture per testbed_recipe.operator_targets. The rationale is accurate and there is no internal inconsistency.",
      "repair_instruction": ""
    }
  ]
}

